course: dictionary
lesson: 9128
title: Smuggle: secret and against the rules
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To smuggle something means to take it somewhere secretly, when that is against the law or the rules. The word carries that secret, sneaky feeling. «Pangolins are among the most smuggled mammals.» The word smuggled tells you these animals are carried out of their homes in secret and against the law.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «China kept the making of silk secret for a long time.» Because the making of silk was a secret, the monks who «smuggled silkworm eggs» had to hide them from the people who guarded that secret. Secretly and against the rules: that is what smuggle means.
explain: The word also has a lighter, everyday meaning. Children can «smuggle» a snack into class, or a pet into the house, when a teacher or parent has said no. Check it by swapping in sneak: «sneak a snack into class» still makes sense, so smuggle here means to sneak something into a place where it is not allowed.
fact: To smuggle means to take goods or people secretly and illegally into or out of a country.
fact: Smuggle is a verb. Its other forms are smuggles, smuggled and smuggling.
fact: In history, smugglers on the coasts of Cornwall hid brandy, tea and tobacco to avoid taxes.
fact: A well-known story says monks smuggled silkworm eggs to the Byzantine Empire around 552 AD, when China guarded the secret of silk.
fact: In everyday life, to smuggle a snack into class means to take it in secretly when it is not allowed.
exercise: «Pangolins are among the most smuggled mammals, killed for their scales.» What does smuggled mean here? | Kept safely in a zoo | Given away as gifts | Counted carefully by scientists | *Taken secretly and illegally
exercise: A story says monks smuggled silkworm eggs to the Byzantine Empire. Why would they need to do it secretly? | Because the eggs were too big to carry | *Because China kept the making of silk secret | Because silk was cheap and everyone had it | Because the monks wanted to sell them in a market
exercise: «Tom tried to sneak a sweet into class.» Find and copy one word from this sentence that means nearly the same as smuggle. | sweet | class | Tom | *sneak
test: «People-smuggling is a serious crime.» What does smuggling mean here? | *Taking people secretly and illegally across borders | Helping people find their way home | Teaching people a new language | Giving people a free ride on a bus
test: Smugglers in Cornwall hid brandy and tea to avoid paying what? | Shouting | Bad weather | *Taxes | Homework
exam: Jayden says smuggle only means taking animals across countries. Is he right? | Yes, only animals can be smuggled | No, it means to buy something cheaply | *No, it can also mean sneaking something into a place where it is not allowed | Yes, but only across the sea
